ir  propaganda also were very adamant that  u.s troops has to be out have to be out  by 1st of may a date that was  conditionally set in the u.s taliban  agreement and also there they have  implied that the overstay would crea  would like be followed by consequences  but then the real politic was this uh  the taliban decided it's not a good idea  to to shoot at the us if they're indeed  on their way out and then they kind of  did nothing um and uh as at least they  didn't attack u.s forces and um  something similar could happen now uh  this is speculation though  now tens of thousands of people have of  course been evacuated from uh the afghan  capital but obviously the pressure is on  now to get more people out you've said  that the desperation the levels of  desperation can't really get much worse  how are things at and around the airport  right now  i have been in touch with someone who  has conducted evacuation like yesterday  night  um and he today in the morning told me  that it is still like celtic scenes  tragic scenes  uh people breaking down  people handing their babies over to two  forces to somehow get them in um so like  really still heartbreaking scenes uh  still a lot of people who desperately  try to get out um in this regard i think  it is important though to also say that  inside the city uh the situation is  relatively calm that doesn't mean that  like people are happy or everything is  fine it just means that like a much  larger number than the tenth of thousand  at the airport um  stay here whether deliberately or if  they kind of  have resigned to the fact that they have  no reasonable way out and they stay  behind and try to sort out their life  now as quietly as calmly as possible  france marty thank you for your  reporting from kabul  now many at-risk afghans are still  waiting to be evacuated from kabul  airport they're mainly interpreters or  contractors who worked for u.s german or  other nato forces but some have already  made it out in 